Let  $P(x,y)$  be the above assertion. Then,  $P(0,y)$  gives  $$ f(y\cdot h(0))=f(0) \quad \forall \, y\in \mathbb R. \qquad (\star) $$  Let's suppose  $h(0)\neq 0$ . 

Then, I claim that  $f$  is constant,  $h(x)=0$  for every non-zero real, and  $h(0)$  can take any real value.
<details><summary>Proof.</summary>By  $(\star)$  we get that  $f$  is constant. But then, observing the original equation, that implies  $x\cdot h(x)=0$  for every real  $x$ . Clearly, the Claim must be true.</details>

Now, let's suppose  $h(0)=0$ . We will distinguish two cases:**Case 1.** There exists a real  $\alpha\neq 0$  satisfying  $f(\alpha)=0$ . 

I claim that the only solutions for Case 1 are  $f$  constant,  $h\equiv 0$ .
<details><summary>Proof.</summary>$P(\alpha,y)$  would give  $$ f(\alpha^2)=f(\alpha y) \quad \forall \, y\in \mathbb R. $$  Hence  $f$  is constant. If  $f$  is constant, then  $x\cdot h(x)=0$  for every real  $x$ , where  $h(0)=0$  by supposition.
The Claim is proved.</details>**Case 2.** There doesn't exist any  $\alpha\neq 0$  satisfying  $h(\alpha)=0$ .

I claim that the only solution to this case will be  $f(x)=x+c$  ;  $h(x)=x$  for every real  $x$ .
<details><summary>Proof.</summary>In that case, then   $$ x\cdot h(x)\neq 0 \quad \forall \, x\neq 0 $$  And observing the original equation, this implies  $$ f(x^2+y\cdot h(x))\neq f(xy) \Longrightarrow x^2+y\cdot h(x)\neq xy \quad \forall x\neq 0 \, , y\in\mathbb R. $$  If such linear equation in  $y$  doesn't have equation, that is because   $$ y=\frac{x^2}{x-h(x)}\notin \mathbb R \quad \forall \, x\neq 0 $$  Therefore,  $(x-h(x))=0$  for every  $x\neq 0$ . But since  $h(0)=0$  by supposition, then   $$ h(x)=x \quad \forall x\in \mathbb R. $$  Thus the original equation is now transformed into   $$ f(x^2+xy)=x^2+f(xy) \quad \forall x,y\in \mathbb R. $$  Let  $Q(x,y)$  be the new assertion. Then,  $Q(x,0)$  gives  $$ f(x^2)=x^2+f(0) \Longrightarrow f(x)=x+c \quad \forall x\in \mathbb R^{+} $$  And  $Q(x,-x)$  gives  $$ f(0)=x^2+f(-x^2) \Longrightarrow f(x)=x+c \quad \forall x\in \mathbb R^{-} $$  Indeed,  $f(0)=c$ , so the Claim is proved.</details>
The three solutions can be grouped as   $$ \boxed{
	\left\{\begin{array}{lll}
		f \thickspace \text{constant,} \thickspace h(x)=0 \quad \forall x\in \mathbb R \setminus \{0\} 
		f(x)=x+c \thickspace ; \thickspace h(x)=x \quad \forall x\in \mathbb R. 
	\end{array}
\right.
} $$

Let  $O$  be the centre of  $S_1$ .
Note that  $S_2$  is the reflection of  $S_1$  over  $AB$ .
It's well-known that the orthocenter of a triangle lies on the reflection of its circumcircle in a side.
It's also known that any circumrectangular hyperbola of triangle passes through the orthocenter of that triangle.
Hence,  $\{F, G\}$  are the orthocenters of  $\{\triangle CAB, \triangle DAB\}$ .

WLOG  $F$  be the orthocenter of  $\triangle CAB$ .
\[CF \perp AB, DG \perp AB \implies CF \parallel DG.\]
\[CF = 2 \cdot \text{dist}(O, AB) = DG\]
Hence,  $CFGD$  is a parallelogram.

