The expression  $c^{3} = a^{3} + b^{3}$  can be rewritten as: $c^{3} = (a + b)(a^{2} - ab + b^{2}) = (a + b)(c^{2} + 2ab \cdot cos(C) - ab)$  (i).

By the Triangle Inequality, we must satisfy  $a + b > c$ .  Thus, let  $a + b = Kc$  (for  $K > 1$ ) and substitute this value into (i) to obtain: $c^{3} = Kc(c^{2} + 2ab \cdot cos(C) - ab)$ ;

or  $c^{2} = K(c^{2} + 2ab \cdot cos(C) - ab)$ ;

or  $(1/K - 1)c^{2} + ab = 2ab \cdot cos(C)$ ;

or  $cos(C) = \frac{(1/K - 1)c^{2} + ab}{2ab}$  (ii).

What follows is a proof by contradiction.  Suppose  $0 < C \le 60^{\circ}$ , which in turn yields  $1/2 \le cos(C) < 1$ . Substituting (ii) into this inequality produces: $1/2 \le [(1/K - 1)c^{2} + ab] / 2ab < 1$ ;

or  $ab \le (1/K - 1)c^{2} + ab < 2ab$ ;

or  $0 \le (1/K - 1)c^{2} < ab$  (iii).

Since the quantity 1 $/K - 1$  is strictly negative, (iii) is only satisfied when  $c = 0$  (a contradiction).  Hence,  $60^{\circ} < C < 180^{\circ}$ .

QED

